.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-VM-IP 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-vm-ip
.Nd the address of a virtual machine, found one way for every kldload tool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar machine
.Nm
.Fl -all
.Op Fl -json
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the IPv4 address a machine on this host can be reached at.
Every kldload tool that needs one asks here, so they all agree.
.Pp
For one
.Ar machine ,
in order:
.Bl -enum
.It
A Firecracker machine made by
.Xr kfire 8 ,
which has no libvirt domain: the address it registered when its guest
answered.
.It
A libvirt domain that is not running has no address, and
.Nm
says so.
.It
The guest agent, asked for at most five seconds: its first IPv4 address
that is not loopback and not on a WireGuard interface \(em a machine on a
mesh reports its mesh address too, and that is not where to reach it
from here.
.It
The DHCP leases of every libvirt network, matched to the machine by the
MAC address its definition gives it \(em not by the host name in the
lease, which a clone copies from its golden \(em the newest lease winning.
.El
.Pp
.Fl -all
does the same for every running domain and every Firecracker machine in
one pass.
.Pp
It needs to read the system libvirt: root, or a member of the
.Ql libvirt
group.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar machine
Print its address.
.It Fl -all
Print
.Ql name address
for every running machine that has one.
.It Fl -json
With
.Fl -all :
one JSON object,
.Ql {"name":"address",...} .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev LIBVIRT_DEFAULT_URI
The libvirt to ask; default
.Ql qemu:///system .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/lib/kfire/instances/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa /instance.json
A Firecracker machine's record, read with
.Xr jq 1 .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
An address was printed.
.It 1
None: the machine is not running, does not exist, or has no address yet;
with
.Fl -all ,
no machine had one.
.It 2
A usage error, or a name that is not a plain name.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30, as a user in the
.Ql libvirt
group, with a throwaway clone,
.Ql vi-3545202 ,
running, then powered off, then deleted.
.Ss One machine
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-vm-ip vi-3545202
192.168.122.47
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss Every running machine (--all, --json)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-vm-ip --all
vi-3545202 192.168.122.47
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-vm-ip --all --json
{"vi-3545202":"192.168.122.47"}
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss A machine that is off
Its lease still exists, and is not an answer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ virsh -c qemu:///system destroy vi-3545202   # (as root)
$ kldload-vm-ip vi-3545202
kldload-vm-ip: vi-3545202 is shut off, so it has no address
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Ss In a script
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ip=$(kldload-vm-ip web01) || { echo "web01 has no address" >&2; exit 1; }
ssh "root@$ip" uptime
.Ed
.Ss What it refuses
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-vm-ip web/1
kldload-vm-ip: 'web/1' is not a domain name
# (exited 2)
$ kldload-vm-ip no-such-vm
kldload-vm-ip: no domain or kfire machine named no-such-vm
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "kldload-vm-ip: MACHINE is STATE, so it has no address"
.It "kldload-vm-ip: no domain or kfire machine named MACHINE"
.It "kldload-vm-ip: virsh is not installed \(em this is not a hypervisor"
.El
A running machine with no address yet \(em still booting, no guest agent,
no lease \(em exits 1 without a message.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr kfire 8 ,
.Xr kldload-estate 8 ,
.Xr kldload-networks 8 ,
.Xr kvm-grow 8 ,
.Xr kvm-mesh 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
was written on 2026-09-26, when six tools each found addresses their own
way \(em one network only, the agent only, leases by host name \(em and
disagreed about the same machine within the same minute.
.Pp
On 2026-09-29, writing this page: a machine that was off was given its
old lease's address, which DHCP hands on to others; run as a user it
asked the user's own, empty, libvirt and found nothing; and usage errors
went to standard output.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
An address is where the machine was last seen, not proof it answers
there; a caller that is about to log in should expect to be refused.
.Pp
Only IPv4 is looked for.
